The standard approach in .NET is to **wrap file system calls behind an abstraction** you can mock in tests:

1. **Define an interface** like `IFileSystem` with methods matching what you use (`ReadAllText`, `WriteAllText`, `DirectoryExists`, etc.).
2. **Create a concrete implementation** that delegates to `System.IO.File` / `System.IO.Directory`.
3. **Inject `IFileSystem`** into classes that need file access via constructor injection.
4. **In tests**, substitute a fake/mock (`Moq`, `NSubstitute`, or a hand-rolled in-memory implementation).

Alternatively, use the built-in `System.IO.Abstractions` NuGet package — it provides `IFileSystem` out of the box along with an in-memory test helper (`MockFileSystem`).

```csharp
// Option A: Roll your own
public interface IFileSystem
{
    string ReadAllText(string path);
    void WriteAllText(string path, string contents);
    bool DirectoryExists(string path);
}

public class PhysicalFileSystem : IFileSystem
{
    public string ReadAllText(string path) => File.ReadAllText(path);
    public void WriteAllText(string path, string contents) => File.WriteAllText(path, contents);
    public bool DirectoryExists(string path) => Directory.Exists(path);
}

// Option B: Use System.IO.Abstractions
// dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
// dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```

**`System.IO.Abstractions`** is the community standard — it covers the full `System.IO` surface so you don't have to grow your own interface over time.
